nesses to have some certainty for  our transitory kind of related  industries like an agriculture meaning  seasonal what have you we need to  this workforce so I think having people  that have unfortunately been living in  the shadows step up I'm okay with some  form of volunteer commitment if they are  to move toward citizenship a fee if that  makes sense I'm willing to negotiate  through those but it's time we move  beyond and not to have such a break and  our norms in this nation yeah I think a  lot of people simply don't understand  the importance of these groups of folks  who have come to the United States in a  number of different industries including  agriculture including the restaurant  industry etc would anybody who does not  are there any individuals outside of  those who would meet daca requirements  that you would say are going to have to  leave well I mean clearly if we have  people who are dealing drugs and our  felons and are you know really impacting  our system in a way that doesn't benefit  it we have to do something with that  group of people we have to figure out  where and how we do that would it  whether it would be putting them behind  bars here in our nation or deporting  them I'm open to looking at those  options but I think citizens working or  people that are working toward  citizenship who would desire that who  are integrated in our communities it's  time for us to get beyond the division  on this and tackle some of these  problems on a bipartisan basis and move  forward one of the areas I'm very  interested in particularly because we've  just had at the national level major  changes made to the system of taxation  that exists both for individuals and for  corporations is to dig into that a  little bit and when your current  opponent Mitt Romney was running for  president there were some pretty  significant disagreements on tax polyMet  policy that he had with Barack Obama so  I'd like to dig into that a little bit  what is your policy 